<ForceField>
  <!-- The nucleophile as a WATER, for the ohH arm of the protonation test
       (D112, M0). The lysH arm uses hydroxide.xml, whose residue OH is one O
       and one H; this is the same site with the proton put back, so the two
       arms differ by where one hydrogen sits and in nothing else.

       Residue NUW rather than HOH or OH. Not HOH, because PDBFile infers a
       standard water's bonds from the atom names O/H1/H2 and would find none
       for ours; not OH, because calling a water "OH" in a paper invites the
       confusion this test exists to resolve. Atom names stay O01/H03 (plus the
       new H04), so every selector in site.yaml and in traj_contacts.py works
       unchanged on both arms: the comparison is atom-name-identical and needs
       no second set of selectors. The O01-H04 bond comes from a CONECT record,
       as the hydroxide's O01-H03 bond already does.

       This file deliberately declares NOTHING but the residue. The types are
       amber14/tip3p.xml's own, so the charges, the Lennard-Jones parameters,
       the O-H bond and the H-O-H angle are all inherited from TIP3P rather
       than restated here - which also means this template is *parameter
       identical* to HOH. That matters: a template with its own types would
       also match all ~15000 bulk waters by bond graph, and OpenMM then refuses
       the whole system with "Multiple non-identical matching templates found".
       Identical matches are accepted. Load this file after amber14/tip3p.xml.

       One consequence to know: rigidWater does not recognise NUW, so its
       H-O-H angle is flexible where bulk water's is constrained (verified -
       rigidWater constrains the bulk water and not this residue). With HBonds
       constraining the O-H bonds, the angle is the only free mode, and 4 fs is
       too coarse for it, so the ohH equilibration runs at 2 fs. In the qmmm
       stage mcube builds the MM system with rigidwater=False and
       autoconstraints=None, so the nucleophile is entirely free there - which
       is what allows the proton to move during QM/MM MD. -->
  <Info>
    <DateGenerated>2026-10-27</DateGenerated>
  </Info>
  <Residues>
    <Residue name="NUW">
